nt": 0.79, "key_factors": ["District's R+7 partisan lean and Crane's incumbency favor the Republican", "Crane holds a large fundraising and cash-on-hand advantage over Nez", "Most expert raters call the race Likely R, with a few at Lean R", "A Democratic-sponsored August poll shows Nez within two points, suggesting a competitive race"], "rationale": "The district's Republican lean, Crane's incumbency and financial edge, and a Likely R consensus among raters point to a Crane win, while markets price Nez's chances around a third. A late Democratic-sponsored poll showing a two-point race and Nez's visibility on water and cost-of-living issues keep this short of Likely R.", "watch": "If additional independent polling confirms the narrow GBAO margin, or Nez's advertising closes the spending gap, the race could shift toward a genuine tossup.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["Incumbent Eli Crane (R) is running."]}, "overview": "First-term Republican Eli Crane defends a northeastern Arizona seat with a seven-point Republican lean against Jonathan Nez, a former Navajo Nation president making this a rematch of their 2024 contest. Crane has raised roughly $9.5 million and holds a cash advantage, and has reserved $2.1 million for fall advertising, while Nez has raised about $3.5 million with little outside support so far.\n\nMost expert raters place the race at Likely Republican, though a few call it Lean Republican. Polling is sparse: a Democratic-sponsored August survey showed Nez trailing by just two points, while a Republican-sponsored poll that month showed an eleven-point Crane lead. Prediction markets give Nez roughly a one-in-three chance. The race leans Republican, with Nez banking on cost-of-living and water issues to overcome the district's fundamentals.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.1703, "margin": -5.61}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.2296, "margin": -5.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.2331, "margin": -5.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_d